## 11. Multilingual Retrieval Rules

Define how retrieval should work across languages.

Retrieval may need to account for:

* user input language
* source document language
* official system language
* translated search queries
* multilingual synonyms
* transliteration
* local terminology
* spelling variations
* mixed-language input

| Retrieval situation                         | Retrieval behavior | Notes     |
| ------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | --------- |
| User language matches source language       | `[Behavior]`       | `[Notes]` |
| User language differs from source language  | `[Behavior]`       | `[Notes]` |
| Query contains official term                | `[Behavior]`       | `[Notes]` |
| Query contains informal term                | `[Behavior]`       | `[Notes]` |
| Query requires transliteration              | `[Behavior]`       | `[Notes]` |
| Retrieval results conflict across languages | `[Behavior]`       | `[Notes]` |

Guidance:

* Preserve original query terms when helpful.
* Add translated or mapped terms only when allowed.
* Label retrieved context language.
* Do not assume translated retrieval results are equivalent without confidence checks.
* Escalate or ask for clarification when multilingual retrieval ambiguity affects workflow decisions.

Example retrieved context language fields:

```yaml
retrieved_context:
  - source: "[Source]"
    source_language: "[Language]"
    query_language: "[Language]"
    translated_query_used: true
    translation_confidence: "[low | medium | high]"
```

