## 12. Transliteration Rules

Define how transliteration should be handled when users type names, places, IDs, or terms in another script.

Examples of situations requiring transliteration rules:

* Arabic place names written in Latin characters
* names with multiple accepted spellings
* service names typed phonetically
* IDs or codes mixed with text
* user-provided addresses

| Situation                                             | Assistant behavior | Confirmation needed?       |
| ----------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | -------------------------- |
| Name has multiple possible spellings                  | `[Behavior]`       | `[Yes / No / Conditional]` |
| Location may map to more than one official place name | `[Behavior]`       | `[Yes / No / Conditional]` |
| User-provided ID includes ambiguous characters        | `[Behavior]`       | `[Yes / No / Conditional]` |
| Informal spelling maps to official term               | `[Behavior]`       | `[Yes / No / Conditional]` |

Guidance:

* Preserve the user’s original spelling.
* Map to official spelling only when confidence is sufficient.
* Ask for confirmation when ambiguity affects routing, eligibility, identity, location, or records.

