## 13. Language Ambiguity Rules

Define what happens when language or meaning is ambiguous.

Ambiguity may occur when:

* a phrase has multiple meanings
* a translated term maps to multiple categories
* the user uses idioms, slang, dialect, or informal wording
* a service term has no direct translation
* a location, name, or ID is unclear
* user intent depends on cultural or local context

| Ambiguity type                              | Assistant behavior | Escalation or review needed? |
| ------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ---------------------------- |
| Multiple possible request categories        | `[Behavior]`       | `[Yes / No / Conditional]`   |
| Unclear urgency                             | `[Behavior]`       | `[Yes / No / Conditional]`   |
| Unclear location or entity name             | `[Behavior]`       | `[Yes / No / Conditional]`   |
| Low-confidence translation                  | `[Behavior]`       | `[Yes / No / Conditional]`   |
| Dialect or informal wording affects meaning | `[Behavior]`       | `[Yes / No / Conditional]`   |

Default guidance:

* Ask a targeted clarification question when ambiguity affects the workflow outcome.
* Proceed with a labeled assumption only when the workflow allows it.
* Preserve original wording for review.
* Do not silently collapse ambiguous terms into a single official category.

